      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 65-year-old man presented to our institution with recurrent episodes of early morning chest discomfort and near syncope. An ergonovine provocation test documented a diagnosis of coronary vasospastic angina. Using our investigation of the syncope, a head-up tilt table test provoked a severe episode of coronary vasospasm that resulted in a life-threatening cardiac event. The present case reminds us that an elevation of ST segments on the electrocardiogram during tilt testing should be promptly managed as an attack of coronary vasospasm."},
